<h1>Dawn: User Guide</h1>

<p class="details">
<span id="author">Přemysl Eric Janouch</span><br>
<span id="email"><a href="mailto:p@janouch.name">p@janouch.name</a></span><br>
<span id="revnumber">version @PROJECT_VERSION@</span>

<h2>Introduction</h2>

<p><i>Dawn</i> is a colour-managed image browser and viewer. This document is
a compact overview of the application as it stands. Press
<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#8239;+&#8239;<kbd>?</kbd> in the program for the current
keyboard shortcut list.

<h2>Controls</h2>

<p>Most commands are on the keyboard. <kbd>F1</kbd> opens this guide in your
web browser. <kbd>F10</kbd> opens the menu, <kbd>F6</kbd> moves between
the toolbar, any sidebar, and the main view, and <kbd>F</kbd> shows Vimium-style
hints for on-screen controls.

<p>Mice with dedicated back and forward buttons work as in a web browser.
The pointer wheel zooms in the viewer and scrolls the browser. Left- or
middle-drag pans an image; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>&#8239;+&#8239;middle-drag zooms;
<kbd>Alt</kbd>&#8239;+&#8239;middle-drag or a pinch gesture rotates.

<h2>Browser</h2>

<p><i>Dawn</i> starts in a directory browser unless you open a file. The left
sidebar lists Computer, Home, Pictures when that folder exists, then
breadcrumbs to the current directory and its children. The toolbar changes
thumbnail size, tiled or grid layout, filename captions, whether unsupported
files are hidden, and the sort order.

<p><kbd>Enter</kbd> opens the selected file in the viewer. Middle-click, or
<kbd>Shift</kbd>&#8239;+&#8239;<kbd>Enter</kbd>, opens a path in a new window.
Right-click a file or the directory for a context menu, including other
applications registered for that type.

<h2>Viewer</h2>

<p><kbd>Enter</kbd> returns to the browser. Files are visited in the same order
and with the same filtering as the directory you came from. Double-click the
image to toggle fullscreen.

<p>Zoom, fit, colour management, smooth scaling, and the transparency
checkerboard are on the toolbar and the View menu. Rotate and mirror are on
the Image menu, together with pages of a multi-page file and frames of an
animation.

<h2>Information</h2>

<p>Show Information opens a sidebar with the file name, pixel size, a CIE
chromaticity diagram for the current colour space, and any text tags the
decoder exposed.

<p><i>Launch ExifTool</i> runs ExifTool on the open file and opens the report
through your default handler for <code>.txt</code> files. The Windows package
ships ExifTool; elsewhere it must be on the search path.

<h2>Thumbnails</h2>

<!-- TODO(p): The path is slighlty different on macOS and Windows. -->

<p><i>Dawn</i> stores wide, colour-managed thumbnails under the XDG thumbnail
cache, typically <i>~/.cache/thumbnails</i>. To drop entries that no longer
match a file, run:

<pre>
dn --invalidate-cache
</pre>

<h2>Configuration</h2>

<p><i>Dawn</i> uses the display colour profile provided by the operating system:
colord on Linux, the profile assigned in Windows Colour Management, or the
profile on the display in macOS System Settings. Assign the profile that
matches the monitor if colours look wrong.

<p>Per-window View toggles such as Dark Mode and Colour Management apply for
the session. Command-line options, including
<code>--new-instance</code> on Linux, are listed by <code>dn --help</code>.

<script>
var toc = '', all = document.querySelectorAll('h2')
for (var i = 0; i < all.length; i++) {
	var name = all[i].innerHTML.replace(/[^0-9a-z]/ig, '-')
	toc += '<li><p><a href="#' + name + '">' + all[i].innerHTML + '</a></li>'
	all[i].id = name
	all[i].innerHTML = (i + 1) + '. ' + all[i].innerHTML
}
all[0].insertAdjacentHTML('beforebegin',
	'<h2>Table of Contents</h2><ol>' + toc + '</ol>')
</script>
